WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.611 --> 00:00:17.910
Well, thanks so much for being
here today for Build Day.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.570
Build Day's really all
about turning great ideas

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.100
into great products,

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.160
and so when we stepped back
and asked what that might mean,

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.640
we thought, well, no better
company to talk to than Vercel,

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.580
who's very much at the forefront
of making that possible,

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.170
and Guillermo, in particular,

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:38.760
who, of course, is the
genius behind Next.js

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.950
and Vercel in parallel.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.511
So, lots of good conversation
to be had around open source-

00:00:43.511 --> 00:00:44.880
Good to be here, thank you.
-And business.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:45.990
I think earlier this year

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.420
you guys announced 200 million ARR

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.420
and a doubling of users year over year.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.640
For those of you who are
reasoning about sort of scale

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:54.900
and what might be possible,

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.410
I heard 4.3 million downloads a week

00:00:58.410 --> 00:00:59.880
of your AI SDK alone.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.550
Yeah, it's hard to keep count of downloads

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.230
'cause they're growing so fast.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.610
Good problem to have.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.770
But, yeah, the business has
been growing really fast,

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.380
and, as many others I think here,

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.760
it was born in the cloud
and it was born on Stripe.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.050
Amazing, and, you know, I think

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.580
with many of today's startups,

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.500
rather than reasoning about
year-over-year growth,

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.780
we're increasingly reasoning
about month-over-month growth

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.649
or even week-over-week growth-
Day.

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:25.091
Day.
Day?

00:01:25.091 --> 00:01:26.670
You could ship something today

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.020
that makes a huge difference.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:29.820
Always elevating ambitions, amazing.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:30.810
Well, thank you for being here.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.780
I thought we could start maybe
a bit with your background.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.330
Born in Buenos Aires,
taught yourself how to code?

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:37.163
True story?

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:37.996
That's right, yeah.

00:01:37.996 --> 00:01:39.720
Curious about kind of your early journey

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.220
and what brought you to the US.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.360
You know, there are a lot of parallels

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.380
between what I set out to do
with Vercel in my early days

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.010
because teaching myself to code

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:54.000
and speak English was
quite the challenge, but-

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.229
You taught yourself English?

00:01:55.229 --> 00:01:56.370
English was not spoken at home?

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.530
The funny story, no.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.990
In fact, my parents were
really interested in it

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:03.870
'cause, in Argentina, like,
the country was always divided

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.960
between like capitalism and socialism.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.600
And my parents were very much
like engineers themselves,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.120
industrial and chemical engineers,

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.940
and they had a lot of admiration
for great engineering.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.160
And they knew that the
cutting edge would be

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.800
in Silicon Valley in the United States,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.680
but you needed to know
English in order to get there.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.740
And so I was interested
in software engineering,

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.080
and I noticed that a lot
of the programming manuals,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.390
documentation, readmes, were in English.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:32.517
So I basically had a
bootstrapping process to do.

00:02:32.517 --> 00:02:34.710
And I tell people these
days that, you know,

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.240
AI is sort of a before and after

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.980
because, if I go back in time

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.630
and I'm trying to teach
myself how to code,

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.310
I can do it in my native language,

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.360
and I can do it at every step.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.690
I can just ask the LLM to teach me.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:47.767
Like, literally, one of the
things with LLMs is like,

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:50.280
"Explain quantum mechanics like I'm five."

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.360
I could have done that.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.369
I could have-
When you were five.

00:02:52.369 --> 00:02:54.210
You could have done that
when you were five, yeah.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:55.260
So I didn't have that.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.830
The next best thing was the internet,

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:02.520
and the next, next best
thing was open source.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.490
So, for me, one of the discontinuities

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.680
in my learning journey
was when I started getting

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.870
to know the open-source communities,

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.730
contributing to open source,

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.170
spending a lot of time connected

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.570
with strangers on the
internet, as one does,

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:16.710
and learning together.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:20.490
And so I started contributing
to a lot of web frameworks.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.250
One of the earliest was called MooTools.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:24.930
It was a JavaScript library,

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.240
and that was actually very formative.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.010
You know, if I think of my resume,

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.980
my resume was a bunch of
the open-source projects

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.480
that I contributed to.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.780
And the web itself was
for me sort of the vehicle

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.540
that allowed me to
basically leave Argentina

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:42.600
and become a worldwide
figure in programming

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.960
because the instant that
I was able to use the web

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.600
to very quickly ship things,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.660
fork something and put it out into URL,

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.420
now you have a worldwide audience, right?

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.310
These hyperlinks can travel at the speed

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.470
of, you know, thought pretty much.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:02.310
So fast-forward to Vercel
and had the same inspiration.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:03.625
If you wanna start a company,

00:04:03.625 --> 00:04:04.830
if you wanna start a digital business,

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:07.800
if you wanna ship your next big idea,

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.810
I need to make it extremely easy.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:11.610
I need to remove all of the friction.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:13.740
And, funny enough when I
was here in Silicon Valley,

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.340
I had already sold my first
business to WordPress,

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.360
I noticed that, you know,
the cloud was all the rage.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.620
I always encourage people to think

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.140
about platform shifts very closely.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.840
Clearly now we have a
platform shift towards AI.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:29.820
Like 10 years ago when I started Vercel,

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.220
the platform shift was towards the cloud.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:35.250
I looked at the cloud, and it was so hard,

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:36.810
even though I had like 10 years

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:38.850
of engineering experience under my belt.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:41.970
And so Vercel set out
to remove all friction,

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.150
from idea to global cloud application.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:49.830
And so you're kind of
developing Next.js and Vercel,

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:52.440
one open source, one is
a business, in parallel.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.080
I'm curious like hard
trade-offs along the way

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.210
when you had to make a
call, one versus the other.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:01.410
Yeah, where there was no
trade-offs was in my vision.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.200
I wanna make the web easy,

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:07.530
and so when I was thinking
about it from first principles,

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.270
something that you guys
love to do here at Stripe,

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:13.530
okay, I sat down, and I needed
two halves of this equation.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:16.260
I needed the tooling, compilers,

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.840
programming language, runtime framework,

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.450
and then I needed the
cloud infrastructure.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:22.890
And so interestingly enough,

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.300
I noticed an asymmetry in the market.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:27.570
I noticed an unmet need

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.150
because most of the platform providers

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:33.390
were leaving the framework
side for you to figure out.

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:35.490
So AWS and Google Cloud and whatever,

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.100
like, here's a bunch of
like virtual machines

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.860
and clusters and networking solutions.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:42.660
Right, you do the rest.

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:44.910
It's like the meme of
draw the rest of the owl.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:46.680
They were like, "Here's some primitives.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.750
You figure it out how to
build an application."

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.280
And so I looked at it
from first principles.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.540
I was like, "Wow, that's strange,"

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:56.540
'cause as I'm in the act of
drawing this proverbial owl,

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:58.800
I'm realizing I need tooling.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.170
And so that's where the
idea of Next.js came from.

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.870
It's like, hey, I wanna
create an application

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:07.740
that is of the
sophistication, performance,

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:11.040
and flexibility of the
giants of the internet

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.740
'cause I always caveat my
little storytelling with,

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.540
look, there were ways to publish

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:17.100
to the internet really easily.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:20.190
You could have, you
know, go to Squarespace

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:22.830
or Wix or Tumblr.

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:25.530
Like, there were ways
to get something online,

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:28.230
but my aspiration was
that you would be born-

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:31.980
much like Stripe-like, your
business is born on Vercel.

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:33.120
It never graduates

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:36.360
because we give you infinite
scale and infinite power.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.390
And so I solved the Next.js
side, made it open source,

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:41.730
and then I also needed to
solve the other equation

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:43.560
with half of the equation, which is,

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:45.930
all right, how do you host and scale this?

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:47.880
And I also set a really
high bar for myself.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:50.880
I wanted to make it
universally performant.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.850
I wanted to make it such that
without even being an expert

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:55.620
in optimization or infrastructure,

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:58.050
you could deploy something that scales

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.490
and performs excellently.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:00.810
And so those were the two things.

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.250
One is more on the business side,

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:06.651
and the other one is sort of
our presentation to the world.

00:07:06.651 --> 00:07:09.210
Our cover letter to the
world is start with Next.js

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:11.790
or any other open-source
frameworks that we contribute to.

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:14.610
I mean, awesome that they
were so hyper-complementary,

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.050
right, additive.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:17.640
Were there hard trade-offs you made?

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:19.170
I mean, even in your own time-
Yes.

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:21.120
Like where you spent
your own time and energy?

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.920
Yeah, it's funny because
I had to overcome a few

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.150
bad, I guess, intuitions
in order to get there.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:31.950
One intuition that I
had at the beginning is:

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:34.320
in order to create the world's best

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:39.320
and most successful startup, I
have to make everybody happy,

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.100
like every single person.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.080
I feel like that's just a life lesson.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.102
In order to be the best,

00:07:44.102 --> 00:07:45.486
yeah, you don't have
to make everyone happy.

00:07:45.486 --> 00:07:46.955
You have to be somewhat disagreeable.

00:07:46.955 --> 00:07:49.109
Yeah, so who did you
agree to disagree with?

00:07:49.109 --> 00:07:50.150
So, interesting,

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:52.170
and this is why like the
story like seems so simple now

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:54.510
that I tell it, it's like,
"Oh, wow, like, genius.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:56.070
Of course, you created the framework

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:57.090
and the infrastructure."

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:59.910
In the beginning, the
infrastructure was so generic

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:02.010
that I was trying to appeal to everybody.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:03.000
You liked Java.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.160
It's like, "Oh, I can't let Emily down.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:06.510
I'll do some Java over here.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:07.830
Oh, and you like Next?

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:09.060
Oh, okay, I'll do some Next.

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:09.893
Oh, and you..."

00:08:09.893 --> 00:08:11.730
And so I really was just beginning

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:14.310
to straddle myself like really thin.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:17.400
And so the principle that
I've now extracted from this

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:18.870
that I'm very happy to
share with the world,

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:20.070
because it can save you some pain,

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:23.490
is your ambition for a
venture-backed business

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:27.210
that'll reach global scale
should be pretty much unbounded.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:28.800
Like, we're here today.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.520
We're gonna colonize Mars
someday, sometime in the future.

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:36.000
So that's the like long-term
aspiration and ambition.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.460
But you also have to meet it with realism

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:41.910
because, if you're just
pure unmitigated ambition,

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:44.040
you're gonna be so up in the clouds-

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:47.670
not the actual cloud, but
the, like, metaphorical cloud-

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:49.500
that you're not gonna get anything done.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:51.810
You might get into a situation
being jack of all trades,

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:55.770
master of none-extremely
dangerous situation to be in.

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:58.620
You might get into the situation
of what some call it like,

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.920
you know, having a
million mediocre customers

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.350
versus 1,000 customers
that really love you.

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:08.310
And so what was obvious in retrospect,

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:09.667
and we did end up doing is,

00:09:09.667 --> 00:09:13.200
"Hey, we'll start by dominating a niche."

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:14.340
So the niche was-

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:16.320
which is funny because
I think a lot of things

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.010
that we believe are
niches turn out not to be-

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:22.650
'cause we've had $250 million of revenue

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:25.230
from a niche, which is front end.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:27.750
First chapter of Vercel was front end,

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:31.650
hosting the front-end side of
websites and web applications,

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:34.320
and then CDN and security services.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:36.840
Our next step, which has come way later

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:40.160
than I would've expected,
is back-end: agents

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:42.270
and the AI cloud, and all those things.

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:44.940
In many ways, you have
to earn the privilege

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:48.900
to expand that product lineup

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:50.250
or that customer base

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:52.860
in order to get to the ultimate
ambition of your business.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:54.870
Actually, we're seeing that
in a lot of the companies

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:55.770
that are being built now.

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:58.830
What feels like a niche with
like a global user base-

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:02.250
and especially sort of like
ability to reach markets

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:04.230
that are well beyond our
own-are not niches at all.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:06.030
They're like very, very large markets.

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:08.004
Okay, but you had to make
hard trade-offs at the start.

00:10:08.004 --> 00:10:09.390
100%.
Had to shoot for the stars

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:11.220
and then decide like which
planet you were gonna land

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:12.053
on first.
100%.

00:10:12.053 --> 00:10:16.680
You talked about like, you
know, the new frontier is AI,

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:18.150
and we've talked a bunch about open source

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:19.200
versus closed source.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:21.300
I'm curious in the context of AI

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:23.430
how you think about open-source models

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:24.660
versus proprietary models,

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:27.420
both in terms of like what
the industry is building,

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:28.253
but also in terms of like

00:10:28.253 --> 00:10:30.810
how developers should adopt the two.

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:32.040
One of the things that I believe

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:36.660
is that once your business
reaches product market fit,

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:38.700
you can't get too satisfied, right?

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.160
Because the world will
expect you to do more.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.260
Your customers will want you to do more.

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:45.690
You'll have the people
and funding to do more.

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:49.137
So you can, you know, expand
into other adjacencies.

00:10:49.137 --> 00:10:50.400
And one of the rules

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:54.000
for when do I expand into my next chapter-

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:57.330
there's a bunch of rules
that I have or principles.

00:10:57.330 --> 00:11:00.480
One is what I call brand permission,

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:03.990
meaning if Vercel
tomorrow launched a washer

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:05.718
and dryer product,

00:11:05.718 --> 00:11:08.490
even if it's slick and
black and beautiful,

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:10.230
you'd be like, "That's a little strange.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:11.550
I was expecting them to launch

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:14.250
maybe like an AI coding tool."

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:16.770
Like your brand doesn't
yet give you permission

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:17.940
to launch a washer-dryer.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:21.060
And also you want to
expand into adjacencies

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:22.830
to your core competence.

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:26.460
So, if you do payments, you
can handle billing next.

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:29.340
Or, if you do payments,
you can handle fraud next

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:30.420
and things like that.

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:32.700
And so, for us, with our new chapter

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:34.800
of becoming the AI cloud,

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:37.140
what we realized is that
there is a lot of demand

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:39.600
for the same things
that we did in the past.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.180
People want simple frameworks, tools,

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:44.940
and solutions for creating agents.

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:48.360
There's like a new
protagonist in this story.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:49.920
I call it, first chapter

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:53.790
of the internet was about pages or pixels.

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:55.650
In fact, if you've used Next.js,

00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:57.570
you know that the very
first thing we asked you

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:00.030
to do was create a pages directory

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:02.070
and toss your files in there.

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:04.800
It's like very much PHP-inspired.

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:06.300
The next chapter-
Pages and pixels

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:07.290
replaced by?

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:08.123
Agents.

00:12:08.123 --> 00:12:12.270
And so agents might not even
have a face, so to speak,

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:16.440
intelligence that is sort of
permeating everything you do,

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:18.060
meeting you in every conversation,

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:20.490
in every platform integrated into Slack,

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:24.030
integrated into Zoom,
integrated into, you know,

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:27.450
parsing your documents while
you're doing other work.

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:29.520
And so this is a completely
different paradigm

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:32.730
of the type of software that
we've all been building.

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:35.430
And this is why it's exciting
to start a business today

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:38.040
because that feels to me
like a platform shift,

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:39.540
and what's interesting
about platform shifts

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:43.140
is that the old thing doesn't disappear.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:46.410
So it's not that tomorrow
there'll be no pixels

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:48.930
or we'll get to a point
where there is no pixels.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:52.140
Much like today, we still
have AM and FM radio,

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:54.750
so the older technology is still there,

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:55.583
and it'll have-

00:12:55.583 --> 00:12:58.170
I think in the case of the web,

00:12:58.170 --> 00:12:59.940
pages and URLs and
links are gonna continue

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:01.560
to have a ton of relevance.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:02.790
But clearly there's a new type

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:04.170
of software people wanna build,

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:05.850
and there's a new opportunity then

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:07.440
to provide the
infrastructure and framework.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:11.010
So just like there was
Next.js, we created the AI SDK.

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:14.130
You called out it has 4.2
million downloads a week.

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:17.010
I think it's actually
gonna surpass Next.js.

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:20.370
Next.js now feels old compared
to like this new hotness.

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:22.860
It's like my new love interest.

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:24.780
No, but they're complementary.

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:26.400
There's like a foundations cloud,

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:28.260
and there's an AI cloud
that you can build on top.

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:30.600
And so whenever you have an idea

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:33.390
for an agent or an assistant,

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:34.740
you will naturally gravitate

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.810
towards a platform that
speaks your language,

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:37.890
and that's what we're building now.

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:40.110
Yeah, so you guys are
really at the forefront

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.150
of helping developers build with AI.

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:43.440
We partnered with you

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:46.710
to make Stripe sandboxes
native inside Vercel.

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:48.600
And I'm curious sort of how you thought

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:52.140
about integrating payments

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:56.160
within your stack and why it
felt sort of worthwhile to you.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.170
Yeah, maybe for some context,

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:00.810
my original ambition transcended Next

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:01.940
or Vercel or the cloud.

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:04.383
It was, okay, if I have a great idea,

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:09.720
how can I convert it into a
productive software entity

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:11.460
or business or whatever?

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:15.420
And so my tool or my inclination was,

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:18.450
look, JavaScript, I
mentioned when I was a kid,

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:21.180
I worked really hard to learn English,

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:23.700
but I also worked really
hard to learn JavaScript.

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:26.020
And what I really liked
about JavaScript was

00:14:28.249 --> 00:14:31.680
it felt like the English of
the programming universe.

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:33.930
Of course, there's lots of languages.

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:35.550
Spanish is not too bad,

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:37.440
but English, if you just learn English,

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:38.700
think about how much you can accomplish.

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:39.750
It's awesome.

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:40.860
Same with JavaScript.

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:42.990
So JavaScript can run on the server side,

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:46.020
client side, edge, build time.

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:48.540
It can be used to
provision infrastructure.

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:51.780
And now our bet is it's
gonna be the main language

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:55.890
of orchestrating AI services,
making API calls to models,

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:58.020
creating agents and workflows, et cetera.

00:14:58.020 --> 00:14:58.853
So that was the inclination,

00:14:58.853 --> 00:15:01.980
is like, "enable the world, broadly."

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:04.890
Now, a little bit of a detour has been,

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:08.790
well, kind of like ironically, perhaps,

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:12.450
English is becoming the next
hot programming language.

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:14.910
So we created this tool v0

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:19.050
where it feels like a self-disruption.

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:21.960
JavaScript allowed us to
enable tens of millions

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:26.160
of people to create, but that's
still a very small number.

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:28.890
How many people can express ideas?

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:32.100
So I'll give you actually
an actual proxy that I use.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:35.700
Slack and Teams have
approximately combined

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:39.240
half a billion monthly active users.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:41.970
What do those people do
when they open Slack?

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:45.870
They, generally speaking,
talk about building software

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:47.730
or building some kind of business,

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:48.637
and they go back and forth.

00:15:48.637 --> 00:15:50.820
"Hey, Emily, how much money
did we make yesterday?"

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:52.680
And she's like, "Well,
what do you mean by money?

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:54.483
Like, tell me exactly
what MRR metric you want."

00:15:54.483 --> 00:15:56.820
It's like, "Okay, see you tomorrow."

00:15:56.820 --> 00:15:58.140
But we go back and forth

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:02.010
in some conversation
around building a business.

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:04.530
Imagine if we could take those words

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:08.100
and actually more directly
and concretely build software.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.650
That was the original idea of v0.

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:13.800
And so for us, it means that
we can take that pool of people

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:17.340
that are in the creator sort of category

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:20.100
from like 20 million to 500 million.

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:22.800
And so we recently integrated Stripe

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:24.660
because obviously what you learn

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:25.890
when people start building software

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:27.120
is that they wanna monetize it.

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:30.600
So we made an integration
such that every person

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:32.940
that puts a prompt into v0

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:35.160
or creates an application into Vercel

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:38.190
has a seamless integration with Stripe

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:39.990
where you don't even need
to set up the environment.

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:42.450
You just get this sandbox provisioned.

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:44.700
And it even surprised me,

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:48.330
but it quickly became our
third-most popular integration

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:49.710
in the entire platform.

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:50.910
It should not be surprising

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:52.830
because anytime you build something,

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:55.680
you need that flywheel of:
is this actually working,

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:57.000
and do people want it?

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.010
And the best thing you
can do is sell that thing

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:00.900
and get feedback from the market.

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:03.210
So it's been amazing
because I think, jointly,

00:17:03.210 --> 00:17:07.080
we're enabling not just the
people that know how to code

00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:08.760
or are proficient in coding,

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:10.470
but now the next generation of,

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:13.650
whether you call them vibe coders or not,

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:16.380
but it goes back to that original mission

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:17.760
of enabling everyone to ship.

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:21.480
Any sort of surprising businesses
that you've seen spin up

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:23.310
with sandboxes like
that you didn't expect,

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:24.720
you know, just from going through?

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:27.360
Well, there's a lot of people

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:30.360
that are building, like, ideas.

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:32.070
They might have a main job,

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:33.540
and then they're like
building sort of their,

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:35.910
like, startup, their dreams on the side.

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:39.750
I feel like I've seen an
amplification of this,

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:44.023
where, you know, for me, when
I was at my previous company,

00:17:44.023 --> 00:17:46.890
I was dreaming of the company
I was gonna create next.

00:17:46.890 --> 00:17:48.383
But I think the barrier-

00:17:48.383 --> 00:17:49.447
But you weren't building it yet.

00:17:49.447 --> 00:17:50.280
The barrier felt too high.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:51.113
It was too hard.

00:17:51.113 --> 00:17:51.946
That's right. That's right.

00:17:51.946 --> 00:17:54.510
And so I think there are
a lot of people now that,

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:56.880
you know, one thing that's
been really surprising

00:17:56.880 --> 00:17:58.650
is younger people.

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:01.950
So obviously, I had this
story-my personal story-of like,

00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:03.480
I had to learn quite a bit

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:05.430
in order to actually become someone

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:07.500
that could sell something online,

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:08.970
just integrating all the services,

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:10.560
programming languages, et cetera.

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:11.400
I've been hearing from people

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:12.817
that are professional
engineers that tell me,

00:18:12.817 --> 00:18:17.250
"Look, my siblings, they're
like 12, 13 years old,

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:19.890
and they're already
selling products online."

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:21.630
So that's also been really surprising.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:24.990
The other one that's even
more surprising, perhaps, is

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:26.940
within enterprises,

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:30.300
the same sort of inclinations are in play

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:32.610
where people have ideas,

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:35.400
but they don't have the
resources to execute.

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:37.380
And so giving them a tool

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:40.260
that can enable fast prototyping...

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:43.950
One that was maybe not related
to payments specifically

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:46.650
but was bewildering to me
is we heard about a company

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:49.750
that went from v0 to patent filed

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:54.810
just through like
prototyping, trying it out,

00:18:54.810 --> 00:18:56.460
going back and forth with customers,

00:18:56.460 --> 00:18:58.470
visualizing the idea, et cetera.

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:01.080
Like, okay, this actually
is a meaningful evolution

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:02.370
of our business.

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:05.340
Without a tool that allowed
them to move this fast,

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:07.560
they need to like get
engineering resources.

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:09.120
They need to do all these things.

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:10.950
So there might be a
renaissance of, you know,

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:12.300
it seemed like the only way

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:14.640
that a enterprise scale could innovate

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:16.980
is by acquiring a startup

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:18.570
or by some people leaving

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:22.200
and starting a startup where
like they were free to innovate

00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:24.510
and use tools and move really fast.

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:25.937
I think there's a version of this

00:19:25.937 --> 00:19:27.450
that's gonna happen
within existing companies,

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:29.700
and it's been exciting
to play a role there.

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:31.140
I can't get out of my head this vision

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:33.990
of like a five-year-old Guillermo,

00:19:33.990 --> 00:19:36.090
doesn't speak English
yet, in Buenos Aires,

00:19:36.090 --> 00:19:39.720
sitting at a v0, you know, computer.

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:40.920
What would you build?

00:19:40.920 --> 00:19:42.450
So it's happening already, right?

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:44.940
So one thing I've been struck by

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:48.060
is people send us like
the really cool things

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:49.710
they build with v0.

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:52.620
They share the chats in
our community, et cetera.

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:54.090
And it's been amazing to see that a lot

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:56.040
of these chats are not in English.

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:58.860
Japanese, Spanish, Arabic,

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:00.694
like, the LLMs just don't care.

00:20:00.694 --> 00:20:02.550
Is it as performant in non-English?

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:04.110
So this is the other thing
that's been shocking.

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.210
So I've only had limited exposure,

00:20:06.210 --> 00:20:08.760
so I haven't done a rigorous analysis,

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:12.210
but from my vibe-ometer,
Spanish is amazing.

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:15.960
So that young Guillermo maybe
would not speak English today

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:17.340
'cause he continued-

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:18.660
Wouldn't have needed to, yeah.

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:19.760
building in Spanish.

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:23.130
But the other thing that's fascinating

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:27.360
is this is not a one-way door, right?

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:29.790
A lot of people are starting with v0

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:33.090
and they're becoming
proficient engineers later.

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:34.920
A lot of people ask me
like, "Do you believe

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:37.740
that this tool replaces
the engineering role?"

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:40.440
I don't think so, and I
already have enough evidence

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:42.067
from seeing people telling me, like,

00:20:42.067 --> 00:20:44.070
"I spent three months in v0.

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:46.650
It opened my mind to the different kinds

00:20:46.650 --> 00:20:49.380
of special skills that
it could develop later.

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:51.630
And now I do this, and I connect to Git,

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:53.133
and I use other tools.

00:20:54.475 --> 00:20:57.210
And I'm going towards that
journey of proficiency."

00:20:57.210 --> 00:21:00.210
I don't think people should
necessarily stop at prompting.

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:03.840
In fact, this is how I
quote, unquote, "code" today.

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:05.340
Whenever I have an idea,

00:21:05.340 --> 00:21:08.970
it's actually way more practical
for me to put it into v0

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:10.740
and send a prototype to my team

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:14.730
than try to express the
same idea either in Slack

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:17.220
or text messages or whatever.

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:19.980
Or, you know, sometimes
the friction to coding

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:23.100
and creating an environment,
it's just too high.

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:25.230
And so I think these tools
will continue to coexist

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:28.647
in order to help people
dream up really big things.

00:21:28.647 --> 00:21:30.390
Okay, so your own developer
experience- Vercel's

00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:32.610
obviously known for an
incredible developer experience.

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:36.480
AI is changing what it
means to be a developer.

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:38.670
What do you think good
developer experience

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:40.350
is gonna look like a year out,

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:41.550
two years out, three years out?

00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:43.200
Yeah, one thing that's definitely changed

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:46.440
in how we operate
internally is that we talk

00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:49.530
of the LLM as our customer.

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:53.460
So it used to be that I designed
Next.js only for people,

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:57.000
and the persona that I
would imagine in my head is,

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:58.860
all right, there's this developer.

00:21:58.860 --> 00:22:01.110
Maybe they've had some exposure to PHP.

00:22:01.110 --> 00:22:03.210
Maybe they know some HTML.

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:05.280
How can I relate to them?

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:08.070
In fact, and I haven't
shared this too broadly,

00:22:08.070 --> 00:22:11.100
but the very first version of Next.js

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:13.830
looked a lot like a templating language

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:17.670
'cause I really badly wanted
to merge the world of React,

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:19.470
which seemed a little divorced

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:22.140
from the mainstream of
programming at the time,

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:24.060
and I wanted to make it super relatable.

00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:26.100
I wanted to make it like MySpace codes.

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:28.470
I wanted to make it like,
"Oh, it just looks like HTML

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.840
with some JavaScript logic on top."

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:34.680
Or even Shopify Liquid looks
a little bit like this.

00:22:34.680 --> 00:22:38.340
And so my customer was that developer.

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:40.800
And, whenever I design something,

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:44.220
I try to think of a very
broad range of skillset.

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:47.580
This has been sort of like a
personal mantra that I have.

00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:51.390
I call it progressive
disclosure of complexity,

00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:54.030
which is actually something
that's really hard to do.

00:22:54.030 --> 00:22:56.460
How many years old is Stripe?

00:22:56.460 --> 00:22:58.317
I mean, it depends when you count from

00:22:58.317 --> 00:23:00.210
but like slightly over a decade.

00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:03.360
Okay, so in ten years,

00:23:03.360 --> 00:23:06.690
a company will add more
and more complexity

00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:09.210
commensurate to the customers that come in

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:10.800
and the deals that they make.

00:23:10.800 --> 00:23:13.590
So, if Stripe is supporting Shopify,

00:23:13.590 --> 00:23:16.260
the needs of Shopify are way more advanced

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:18.810
than the needs of a one-person shop.

00:23:18.810 --> 00:23:20.940
So, how do you maintain the simplicity

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:22.770
of the early days, when it was mostly used

00:23:22.770 --> 00:23:25.050
by one-person shops, ten years in?

00:23:25.050 --> 00:23:28.350
And so my personal rule
is, one, it's possible.

00:23:28.350 --> 00:23:30.330
It requires a ton of work,

00:23:30.330 --> 00:23:32.520
but the rule is progressive disclosure

00:23:32.520 --> 00:23:34.020
of complexity or sophistication,

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:36.390
meaning you still should present yourself

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:39.960
where you meet the needs and complexity

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:42.000
and experience of that customer

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:43.230
that comes in through the door.

00:23:43.230 --> 00:23:46.230
And then over time, if needed,

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:49.500
you reveal your more complex self.

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:51.780
And so I think there's a
version of that happening now

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:55.290
where, in order for me to
make a product that is good

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:58.200
for one person or a one-person team

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:00.900
or we now host two of the top 20 websites

00:24:00.900 --> 00:24:02.460
on the internet by traffic,

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:04.800
I need to be very, very cognizant

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:07.080
of how do I expose the
complexity to the end user.

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:09.870
But, again, that end user
is no longer just a human.

00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:12.150
We think of the agent

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:15.000
and how is the agent
gonna reason through this.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:17.040
Little example from literally last night.

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:20.370
One of our framework builders that works

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:23.850
on what do we call agentic
developer experience,

00:24:23.850 --> 00:24:25.920
he said, "Look, what happens

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:29.790
when agents use Next.js

00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:34.110
is that they will boot up lots
of dead servers and kill them

00:24:34.110 --> 00:24:36.600
and boot up lots of dead
servers and kill them."

00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:39.300
And this is not a behavior
that a human does.

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:41.070
A human boots up one.

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:42.567
They go to work to their
tech center, that's it.

00:24:42.567 --> 00:24:44.310
And, in some ways, agents are better

00:24:44.310 --> 00:24:46.170
because they try lots of
things really quickly,

00:24:46.170 --> 00:24:47.730
and if you give them rope,

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:50.100
they'll do a lot of stuff in parallel.

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:51.930
And so what we realized is that

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:54.600
in one error message in Next.js,

00:24:54.600 --> 00:24:57.330
we had to include the process ID

00:24:57.330 --> 00:25:02.330
in order to give agent context
of what process to kill

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:04.590
in order to recover the port

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:06.480
that the previous process was using.

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:08.730
This tiny amount of information-

00:25:08.730 --> 00:25:11.610
hashtag and then
identifier of the process-

00:25:11.610 --> 00:25:16.080
is enough to boost the developer
experience, not for people,

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:17.610
maybe actually marginally for people

00:25:17.610 --> 00:25:19.560
'cause like what I would do
if I was an engineer is like,

00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:22.350
oh, I would run PS and then grab my Next

00:25:22.350 --> 00:25:23.850
and then find the process and whatever.

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:25.140
Like, not a huge difference

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:28.650
for people can make a gigantic
difference for agents.

00:25:28.650 --> 00:25:32.730
And so, as developer
tools continue to advance,

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:36.510
they will continue to cater
to this LLM that calls tools

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:38.670
and commands rather than people.

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:43.620
The people will still be behind
the agent instructing it.

00:25:43.620 --> 00:25:46.140
The other metaphor that
I use is that, you know,

00:25:46.140 --> 00:25:48.990
instead of thinking of
yourself as a junior engineer

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:50.310
that joins a company,

00:25:50.310 --> 00:25:53.010
you're basically like a
junior engineering manager

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:53.843
that joins a company,

00:25:53.843 --> 00:25:56.460
and what you're managing
is a fleet of agents.

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:58.230
I like successively revealing complexity.

00:25:58.230 --> 00:26:00.450
I'm wondering if that works
in personal life as well.

00:26:00.450 --> 00:26:01.980
Yeah, I meet you

00:26:01.980 --> 00:26:05.760
and we start talking about
something basic, shared,

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:08.710
and then we can end up talking
about like colonizing Mars.

00:26:08.710 --> 00:26:10.680
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:26:10.680 --> 00:26:12.870
Let's talk about business models.

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:15.690
Business models in the
AI era are different,

00:26:15.690 --> 00:26:16.770
unique, complex.

00:26:16.770 --> 00:26:17.850
I'm curious how you've thought

00:26:17.850 --> 00:26:21.180
about Vercel's own monetization
strategy, business model,

00:26:21.180 --> 00:26:22.620
and kind of what you're
seeing in the market

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:24.660
with all the businesses being built on v0.

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:27.960
Yeah, when you engineer platforms,

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:30.630
some of the early decisions that you make

00:26:30.630 --> 00:26:33.453
can be highly regrettable in the future.

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:36.480
One of them is how you structure.

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:37.313
I think a lot of people

00:26:37.313 --> 00:26:39.300
that have built products
might relate to this,

00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:43.230
for example, how you
structure users, teams,

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:47.130
organizations, workspaces,
roles, et cetera.

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:48.570
You make some decisions,

00:26:48.570 --> 00:26:49.950
and then you wake up five years later,

00:26:49.950 --> 00:26:52.200
and you're like, "Oh, I wish
I'd done it differently."

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:54.540
And I think there's a version
of this with pricing models.

00:26:54.540 --> 00:26:57.450
So if young, five-year-old Guillermo

00:26:57.450 --> 00:26:58.627
in Argentina was asking me today like,

00:26:58.627 --> 00:26:59.550
"What advice do you have

00:26:59.550 --> 00:27:03.450
for like how I should structure
my pricing?" I would say-

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:05.010
I feel like that's a question
you might have asked.

00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:06.000
Yeah, totally,

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:07.722
especially because I would
be chatting with the LLM,

00:27:07.722 --> 00:27:08.555
and the LLM would be like,

00:27:08.555 --> 00:27:09.720
"Yeah, you have to think about this."

00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:12.450
But so, for a couple rules of thumb.

00:27:12.450 --> 00:27:15.930
So one is optimizing for
high-iteration velocity,

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:17.430
not just of your product,

00:27:17.430 --> 00:27:19.920
but your pricing and packaging
and the way you bill.

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:22.770
Like, it's gonna undergo constant change.

00:27:22.770 --> 00:27:26.130
Another one is optimizing
for transparency.

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:28.650
One of the big weapons that
Vercel has today relative

00:27:28.650 --> 00:27:30.540
to old incumbents

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:32.880
is that you can buy
anything off the shelf,

00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:35.700
and then we transparently tell
you how much it costs down

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:36.810
to a very specific unit,

00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:40.170
request, data transfer unit, token of AI.

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:44.370
And the world has very much
evolved to this way of buying,

00:27:44.370 --> 00:27:47.550
where you have a session
with an agent like v0,

00:27:47.550 --> 00:27:49.110
and then we tell you like,

00:27:49.110 --> 00:27:51.150
this part of the conversation

00:27:51.150 --> 00:27:53.850
literally costs you this many credits

00:27:53.850 --> 00:27:57.690
because you used up this
much of intelligence.

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:02.400
So, we very much live in a world
where everything is on tap.

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:05.220
Intelligence is on tap and power.

00:28:05.220 --> 00:28:07.950
I think of cloud computing as power.

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:11.130
I almost think of what
Vercel does as a utility.

00:28:11.130 --> 00:28:14.400
You want us to scale to
infinite concurrency.

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:17.400
So, for example, we host all
of the prediction markets.

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:20.930
We host Polymarket, Kalshi,

00:28:20.930 --> 00:28:22.500
and a bunch of the crypto ones.

00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:25.020
Today they're probably
having a massive day.

00:28:25.020 --> 00:28:26.640
Why? Because everyone's hitting refresh

00:28:26.640 --> 00:28:28.380
on the New York election.

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:30.480
I'll probably do it
when I leave this room.

00:28:30.480 --> 00:28:31.740
Hey, I could see you're antsy.

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:32.853
You wanna know what's happening.

00:28:32.853 --> 00:28:35.640
And so Vercel is
basically letting you draw

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:37.680
as much power as you need.

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:40.470
And we do it in a way that
they didn't need to call 'em

00:28:40.470 --> 00:28:41.850
and say, "Hey, there's
an election coming up.

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:43.500
Can you just give me a
little bit more power, bro?"

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:45.270
No, it's like on demand,

00:28:45.270 --> 00:28:46.710
and that means that we need to optimize

00:28:46.710 --> 00:28:49.263
for a system of enormous transparency,

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:52.200
real-time accounting.

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:53.310
So you can go to Vercel

00:28:53.310 --> 00:28:55.230
and-at any time, in real time-see,

00:28:55.230 --> 00:28:57.090
all right, how many requests,

00:28:57.090 --> 00:29:00.570
how many compute microseconds.

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:02.100
Same for the build process.

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:04.650
So, if you have 100
engineers or one engineer,

00:29:04.650 --> 00:29:08.760
we can provision one build
unit or 100 build units.

00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:10.020
Your team grows.

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:11.820
You can draw more compute.

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:14.100
The same is happening with tokens, right?

00:29:14.100 --> 00:29:16.020
There's some engineers at your company

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:19.830
that will be having very long
sessions with the agents.

00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:21.900
There's some that are
gonna be more parallel.

00:29:21.900 --> 00:29:24.480
There's some people
that, you know, frankly,

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:26.610
as much as I am agent-pilled,

00:29:26.610 --> 00:29:29.070
I have some engineers working
in some frontier domains

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:32.670
where AI gives them no returns today,

00:29:32.670 --> 00:29:35.070
people working on very advanced compiler

00:29:35.070 --> 00:29:35.970
or cloud infrastructure

00:29:35.970 --> 00:29:38.370
that there's just not
enough data or whatever.

00:29:38.370 --> 00:29:42.780
And so you need this very
fluid model of accounting,

00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:43.920
billing, et cetera.

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:47.460
And so what I would be thinking today

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:48.960
when you think about your pricing model

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:51.150
and how you monetize is-

00:29:51.150 --> 00:29:52.920
especially if there is
an intelligence component

00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:54.570
or a power component-

00:29:54.570 --> 00:29:57.840
how do you do some of that
real-time transparency

00:29:57.840 --> 00:29:59.070
back to your customer?

00:29:59.070 --> 00:30:02.550
But also, the SaaS models
are still very relevant.

00:30:02.550 --> 00:30:06.300
There's a lot that is
rightfully metered by seat

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:09.630
or by project or by
some other kind of unit.

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:12.510
So, where Vercel exists today
is kind of a hybrid world

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:14.460
of this very much on demand

00:30:14.460 --> 00:30:16.470
and also for some things
that are more related

00:30:16.470 --> 00:30:19.560
to collaboration, other
aspects of building software,

00:30:19.560 --> 00:30:21.240
more traditional like you would pay

00:30:21.240 --> 00:30:23.820
for GitHub or Figma or others.

00:30:23.820 --> 00:30:26.940
And, of course, Stripe has
evolved to meet this level

00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:28.080
of complexity.
Yeah, we do.

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:30.090
We see a lot of this hybrid
billing model, right?

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:32.130
You want the sweet, sweet ARR,

00:30:32.130 --> 00:30:33.930
but you also don't wanna
charge the same amount

00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:35.070
to the person who's using, you know,

00:30:35.070 --> 00:30:36.960
in browser base like
a couple hours a month

00:30:36.960 --> 00:30:39.000
as the person who's using
thousands of hours a month.

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:41.640
And, I'll tell you, sometimes
entrepreneurs can get

00:30:41.640 --> 00:30:43.500
into a little bit of a cold-start problem

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:45.360
because if you do everything on demand,

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:46.830
then you might have 100,000 customers

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:49.140
that pay you like 3 cents a month.

00:30:49.140 --> 00:30:51.090
And you have fixed platform costs.

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:51.930
You have platform costs

00:30:51.930 --> 00:30:55.140
because you had to like get
something up and running

00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:58.470
in order to just be online
and serve your user base.

00:30:58.470 --> 00:30:59.870
And that's why I'm actually,

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:03.510
number one, I'm a fan of
extreme experimentation.

00:31:03.510 --> 00:31:05.130
I think no one really knows the answer

00:31:05.130 --> 00:31:06.060
to these hard problems,

00:31:06.060 --> 00:31:08.070
and so you have to get out
there and try many things.

00:31:08.070 --> 00:31:10.620
But the other one is that I
think I'm quite open-minded

00:31:10.620 --> 00:31:12.370
about like more of the hybrid ways,

00:31:13.885 --> 00:31:15.600
and obviously it's great that, you know,

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:17.460
you have a lot of the
infrastructure already built.

00:31:17.460 --> 00:31:21.300
So doing it for yourself
I do not recommend.

00:31:21.300 --> 00:31:22.320
It's quite hard.

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:23.940
I wanna jump back to
something you said earlier

00:31:23.940 --> 00:31:25.680
about like the web
today is built on pages.

00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:26.670
Pages aren't going anywhere,

00:31:26.670 --> 00:31:28.830
but there's kind of a
new dominant interface

00:31:28.830 --> 00:31:29.820
that's popping up.

00:31:29.820 --> 00:31:31.170
We didn't really talk about...

00:31:31.170 --> 00:31:32.670
We talked a little bit about
what that's gonna demand

00:31:32.670 --> 00:31:33.503
from the infrastructure layer.

00:31:33.503 --> 00:31:34.980
We didn't really talk about
what that's gonna demand

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:36.420
in terms of new protocols,

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:38.610
and I'm curious sort of
what's top of mind for you.

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:41.940
Yeah, one thing I mentioned briefly is

00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:46.940
if the programming language
of tomorrow is human,

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:50.160
that means that you'll
likely be integrated

00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:51.360
into a lot of the places

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:53.070
where the conversations
are already happening.

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:56.010
So, if you and I are chatting on WhatsApp,

00:31:56.010 --> 00:31:59.220
then we might create a group

00:31:59.220 --> 00:32:01.650
with a bot that is there in WhatsApp,

00:32:01.650 --> 00:32:04.290
or if we're in Slack, same thing.

00:32:04.290 --> 00:32:06.600
So, the interface

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:09.720
that has been the poster child

00:32:09.720 --> 00:32:12.840
of this first era of AI is conversation,

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:15.360
in talking to these agents, in prompting,

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:17.220
and I expect that to continue.

00:32:17.220 --> 00:32:19.650
What's also true is that a lot

00:32:19.650 --> 00:32:22.200
of these platforms already exist.

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:23.910
So there's already ChatGPT.

00:32:23.910 --> 00:32:25.140
There's already Grok.

00:32:25.140 --> 00:32:27.030
There is already v0.

00:32:27.030 --> 00:32:28.740
There's already Cursor.

00:32:28.740 --> 00:32:31.080
And so paying attention to the protocols

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:33.900
that allow you to meet
customers where they are.

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:36.240
Literally, this morning I
was getting a really nice-

00:32:36.240 --> 00:32:37.560
I was telling you about it-

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:40.740
pitch about a data AI company.

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:42.900
And the founder's making the case

00:32:42.900 --> 00:32:44.820
that one of the key interfaces

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:46.890
for his product is gonna be the MCP server

00:32:46.890 --> 00:32:47.880
because a lot of companies

00:32:47.880 --> 00:32:50.610
are building their own AI interfaces.

00:32:50.610 --> 00:32:52.140
I meet a lot of enterprises

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:54.960
that have basically their
own internal ChatGPTs.

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:56.490
We have one at Vercel.

00:32:56.490 --> 00:32:57.750
You guys have one.

00:32:57.750 --> 00:32:59.220
Shopify has one.

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:00.795
And so your product

00:33:00.795 --> 00:33:03.240
might not have a pixel interface, per se.

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:04.627
You might just come to
the company and say,

00:33:04.627 --> 00:33:09.060
"Hey, Stripe, if you connect
to our MCP server over here,

00:33:09.060 --> 00:33:11.490
you're gonna get this value from us."

00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:13.893
So, I'm paying a lot of attention to MCP.

00:33:15.090 --> 00:33:17.160
We have a lot of customers in e-comm.

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:19.140
Black Friday is gonna
be a huge day for us,

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:20.880
also for Stripe, of course.

00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:22.957
And a lot of the questions that I get are,

00:33:22.957 --> 00:33:25.320
"How do I survive and thrive

00:33:25.320 --> 00:33:27.960
in an age of agentic browsers?"

00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:31.567
Meaning, you know, the
customers tells it like,

00:33:31.567 --> 00:33:33.570
"Get me this beautiful black sweater."

00:33:33.570 --> 00:33:34.956
It's a beautiful black sweater.

00:33:34.956 --> 00:33:37.110
Do you think agent commerce
is overrated or underrated?

00:33:37.110 --> 00:33:39.360
I thought you were gonna ask
me, "Where do you get it?"

00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:40.801
No, I don't need one, but it looks-

00:33:40.801 --> 00:33:42.090
I don't think agent commerce is overrated

00:33:42.090 --> 00:33:44.220
because I think, at least for myself,

00:33:44.220 --> 00:33:45.210
I always get the same things,

00:33:45.210 --> 00:33:47.793
the same black shirts 200 times a year,

00:33:48.667 --> 00:33:51.600
and so there is a kind
of purchase that you make

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:52.850
that is very predictable.

00:33:54.690 --> 00:33:57.750
Again, context is king, always has been,

00:33:57.750 --> 00:33:59.700
even more so now within in the age of AI.

00:33:59.700 --> 00:34:00.990
That little example that I gave you

00:34:00.990 --> 00:34:03.690
of like we improved the
Next.js error message

00:34:03.690 --> 00:34:05.250
by five characters,

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:08.040
and all of a sudden, like,
the world works way better-

00:34:08.040 --> 00:34:10.230
the same is true for
how you bring context.

00:34:10.230 --> 00:34:12.480
That's just a way of
doing context engineering.

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:13.980
The concept that we put into the agent

00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:15.540
determines the quality of the outcome.

00:34:15.540 --> 00:34:18.270
For commerce, I think if
you have really good context

00:34:18.270 --> 00:34:22.140
in like what my preferences are
and my history of purchases,

00:34:22.140 --> 00:34:23.820
there's a lot that you can automate.

00:34:23.820 --> 00:34:26.910
I expect it to play a role
even in the world of pixels

00:34:26.910 --> 00:34:28.890
because what a lot of merchants-

00:34:28.890 --> 00:34:30.630
the dragon that they've been chasing

00:34:30.630 --> 00:34:32.400
and have never quite gotten to-

00:34:32.400 --> 00:34:35.610
is that you open favoriteshop.com,

00:34:35.610 --> 00:34:37.500
and it knows exactly what you want

00:34:37.500 --> 00:34:39.840
instead of a generic static page.

00:34:39.840 --> 00:34:41.460
This is literally why Vercel like...

00:34:41.460 --> 00:34:43.380
A lot of our business,
why it's so successful,

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:46.770
is we helped people not ship static pages

00:34:46.770 --> 00:34:49.650
and dynamically render and personalize.

00:34:49.650 --> 00:34:51.240
But I think a lot of these pages

00:34:51.240 --> 00:34:53.220
and interfaces are gonna be LLM-backed.

00:34:53.220 --> 00:34:57.120
So, imagine behind the
scenes of producing a page,

00:34:57.120 --> 00:35:00.600
there's an LLM with a lot of context.

00:35:00.600 --> 00:35:03.060
And imagine that the tool
calls that it can make

00:35:03.060 --> 00:35:06.060
is the orchestration
of your UI components.

00:35:06.060 --> 00:35:09.000
So, all of a sudden when I
go to, you know, keith.com,

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:10.890
the LLM is producing the page.

00:35:10.890 --> 00:35:12.180
That's the mental model that I have.

00:35:12.180 --> 00:35:14.760
Now, some people are
gonna go to keith.com.

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:17.400
Some people are gonna go to chatgpt.com.

00:35:17.400 --> 00:35:18.870
And so you need both.

00:35:18.870 --> 00:35:22.140
You need the protocol to
integrate it into big chat,

00:35:22.140 --> 00:35:24.930
and you also need to,
yourself as a business

00:35:24.930 --> 00:35:26.610
but also the way that you
should think about it,

00:35:26.610 --> 00:35:28.860
is empowering the businesses

00:35:28.860 --> 00:35:31.410
that also wanna maintain
their own interfaces

00:35:31.410 --> 00:35:32.250
with the customer.

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:33.949
And with this new customer that's the LLM,

00:35:33.949 --> 00:35:34.782
both at the same time?

00:35:34.782 --> 00:35:36.087
Yeah, the customer might be an LLM.

00:35:36.087 --> 00:35:38.130
The customer might be a traditional user.

00:35:38.130 --> 00:35:40.560
The way that I think about it
is when I go to the airport,

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:43.200
and I walk past my favorite store,

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:44.970
I might still be inclined

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:46.680
to have a human-centric experience.

00:35:46.680 --> 00:35:48.630
So I walk in and I explore,

00:35:48.630 --> 00:35:50.970
and ideally I would want
that to be hyper-personalized

00:35:50.970 --> 00:35:52.110
and tailored to me.

00:35:52.110 --> 00:35:54.090
Another interface will
be the agent browser,

00:35:54.090 --> 00:35:56.340
meaning I tell it like, "Get
me my black sweater again,"

00:35:56.340 --> 00:35:58.263
and it just goes off and gets it.

00:35:59.490 --> 00:36:03.840
And then the other interface
will just be purely the agent

00:36:03.840 --> 00:36:05.820
that you're not directly interfacing with

00:36:05.820 --> 00:36:07.080
but some other agent.

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:09.210
So this is kind of the
agent-to-agent model.

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:11.400
There's an e-commerce agent over here,

00:36:11.400 --> 00:36:13.110
but I'm interfacing with some other agent

00:36:13.110 --> 00:36:14.490
that in order to fulfill its purpose,

00:36:14.490 --> 00:36:15.810
it needs to buy something.

00:36:15.810 --> 00:36:16.710
We did a little demo.

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:17.970
I mean, this sounds like science fiction,

00:36:17.970 --> 00:36:21.060
but we did a little demo
of this with Stripe,

00:36:21.060 --> 00:36:23.400
where imagine that you're coding,

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.290
and you're talking to a coding agent,

00:36:25.290 --> 00:36:27.570
and you tell it, "Deploy."

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:30.810
And the agent-you haven't made
a deployment decision yet-

00:36:30.810 --> 00:36:32.340
the agent says, "Well,
in order to deploy it,

00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:35.047
I'm gonna use Vercel
'cause it's the best."

00:36:35.047 --> 00:36:39.510
How does that agent successfully
procure a Vercel account

00:36:39.510 --> 00:36:42.270
and pay for it, so that what it gives you

00:36:42.270 --> 00:36:44.040
is actually the outcome that you wanted?

00:36:44.040 --> 00:36:46.860
You wanted a live URL or
you wanted a deployment,

00:36:46.860 --> 00:36:50.130
and it just fulfilled the
commercial transaction

00:36:50.130 --> 00:36:51.870
as an implementation detail.

00:36:51.870 --> 00:36:54.090
Obviously, you will want oversight.

00:36:54.090 --> 00:36:57.750
You want your payment methods
to be, you know, persisted.

00:36:57.750 --> 00:36:59.250
You want fraud prevention.

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:01.440
You want all the good things of buying,

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:03.990
but think of it as like my
assistant is doing the purchase.

00:37:03.990 --> 00:37:07.230
I mean, there's disproportionate
talk of agentic commerce

00:37:07.230 --> 00:37:09.030
in the B2C context,

00:37:09.030 --> 00:37:12.420
but I think the B2B is
far under-discussed,

00:37:12.420 --> 00:37:15.390
and procurement could look
very different a year from now.

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:17.850
Amazing, that is all
we have time for today,

00:37:17.850 --> 00:37:20.460
and a huge thank you to Guillermo
for the insights shared.

00:37:20.460 --> 00:37:22.440
I know I'm walking away with a lot

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:24.213
and hope you all are too.
- Thank you all.

